schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Taxonomy (knowledge organization)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A hierarchy of categories used to file and find things — each item sits
    under a broader category. The formal (SKOS) version gives each concept one
    preferred label and any number of alternate labels (synonyms), with
    broader/narrower/related relations.
  source_traditions:
    - library and information science
    - controlled-vocabulary / thesaurus practice
    - SKOS (W3C linked-data standard)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Recommended default for knowledge commons: build the taxonomy in SKOS
    first (prefLabel/altLabel/broader/narrower), reach for an ontology only
    when a specific recurring task needs machine reasoning.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - "folksonomy (uncontrolled, user-generated tagging — taxonomy is controlled)"
    - "category tree / folder hierarchy (informal analogue)"
  important_distinctions:
    - "taxonomy classifies and enables navigation; it does not encode reasoning rules (that's ontology's job)"
    - "a taxonomy concept has ONE preferred label + alternates — this is what stops label drift (e.g. 'grants'/'funding'/'money')"
  tensions:
    - "simplicity/accessibility (non-technical teammates can read and extend it) vs. expressive limits (no inference)"
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Treating every useful adjective or tag as if it were a taxonomy type
    causes the same "ontology sprawl" problem taxonomies are meant to avoid —
    the type/tag distinction has to be actively maintained, not assumed.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/ontology-vs-taxonomy.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
